First, a quick summary of what’s happening:

– In reST, a single‐backtick interpreted text immediately followed by “`_” (as in  
  `` `text <url>`_ `` ) is an *inline hyperlink*, not “italic text”.  
– In the current Prism grammar, however, the big catch–all inline rule that matches backticks runs *before* the link rules, so `` `ALTER ROLE <…>`_ `` is picked up as just “interpreted text” (and then styled like italic) rather than as a link.  
– When you break it across two lines, the inline rule no longer matches, so it falls through to the link rule and you see the correct styling.

To fix this, we need to teach the grammar to recognize that pattern first. The simplest thing is to insert a small, *very specific* rule *ahead* of the generic backtick rule, one that only matches backtick‐angle‐bracket‐underscore links. That way Prism will tokenize that bit as a link, and leave the rest to the existing link logic.

